# KNOWN GAP — MEMORY UNSAFE, BOTH BACKENDS. A `Callable` stored as a VECTOR
# ELEMENT and called with a STRUCT-typed `&` argument SIGSEGVs, while the
# identical program with an `int` element type runs correctly.
#
# ⚠ THIS IS THE CELL THE IN-CORPUS FIXTURE MISSES. `tests/fixtures/callable_amp_arr_indexed_callee.gg`
# pins the same shape with `Callable[void(int &)]` and is GREEN — but only
# accidentally: a scalar pointee was never subject to the by-value guess.
# Flip ONLY the element type to a struct and the cell dies. Verify:
#     gg build tests/fixtures/callable_amp_arr_indexed_callee.gg   # runs
#     gg build tests/fixtures/known_gaps/callable_amp_vector_element_struct_segv.gg  # rc 139
#
# MECHANISM (measured with `gg build --emit-gir`): the element's GIR type is
# `fn() -> i64` — a ZERO-PARAM FnPtr — because the container-element inferrer's
# signature lookup misses and falls back. So the callee's declared ownership is
# absent from BOTH channels the indirect-call ABI write site reads (the
# operand's `FnPtr::param_ownerships` is empty, and no declared ABI is published
# for this call site), and the backends fall back to guessing from the
# argument's shape — which is the R43 Track C defect, surviving here because the
# fact never reaches the write site. The pointer IS formed correctly
# (`_7 = borrow_mut _4`); only the ABI tag is missing.
#
# Fix belongs at the element-type inferrer: the callable alias signature must be
# populated for `Vector[Callable[...]]` element types. Filed in TODO.md.
#
# RED-VERIFIED at HEAD (with the indirect-call ABI write site landed):
#     C    -> rc 139 (SIGSEGV)
#     LLVM -> rc 139 (SIGSEGV)
#     self-host driver -> rc 139
#
# INTENDED: `2`.
struct P:
    int n

void bump(P &p):
    p.n = p.n + 1

int main():
    Vector[Callable[void(P &)]] arr = []
    arr.push(bump)
    P p = P(1)
    arr[0](&p)
    print(p.n)
    return 0
